          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            A
            %strong (global) choice operator
            is structure included in some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            which provides a uniform way to choose an element of every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
          %p
            In
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Hilbert"} Hilbert
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(x)
            we can form a term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon x.P(x)
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi ε
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists x.P(x) \;\Rightarrow\; P\big(\varepsilon x.P(x)\big).
          %p
            In other words, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            holds of anything at all, then it holds of the particular term
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ε
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon x.P(x)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"} Bourbaki
            and appears in
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                FMathL
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/FMathL"} ?
          %h3#in_category_theory In category theory
          %p
            In category theory, there are two different notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a category. These different notions of logic leads to different notions of a choice operator in category theory, an external choice operator and an internal choice operator
          %h4#external_choice_operator External choice operator
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+category"} well-pointed
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            to behave as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
